  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 117 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will allow for some better mid-level moisture \nas we head into the weekend. A combination of the monsoon plume and \ntransported moisture from Hurricane Marie will allow for largely \nscattered showers/thunderstorms Saturday afternoon. Best chances for \nmore widespread convection will be over the southern Sangres, and \npossible the Raton Mesa as well. Will have to keep an eye on flash \nflooding potential over Aspen Acres, though coverage up that way \nshould be more isolated. Meanwhile, the plains will remain largely \ndry north of the Highway 50 corridor.\n\nMoisture across the region increases Sunday-Monday as the upper high \nshifts and more shortwave energy starts to move over the Rockies. \nThunderstorm coverage will increase both days, especially over the \nContinental Divide. This will also increase our flash flooding \nchances over the burn scars. Temperatures will remain above average, \nif a few degrees cooler thanks to increased cloud cover.\n\nModel solutions diverge as we move towards the middle of next week. \nGeneral patterns show an upper low digging down from the Pacific NW \nand into CA, slowly kicking the upper high towards the east. Speed \nand evolution of this pattern vary between models, though the \ngeneral trend so far leans us in a wetter and slightly cooler \ndirection.",
